  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The fifth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{5}(x)=1-\\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{1}{24}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}-\\frac{1}{96}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})?$",
  "worked_solution": "The coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{4!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{4!} & =\\frac{1}{24} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{24} & =\\frac{1}{24} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
